    private fun story2() = EnStory(
        storyId = "adv_of_mice_and_men",
        chapters = listOf(
            EnChapter(1, "The Two Travelers", listOf(
                "A few miles south of Soledad, the Salinas River drops in close to the hillside bank and runs deep and green, and on its banks two men were walking in single file along the sandy path that led toward the ranch where they hoped to find work.",
                "The first man was small and quick, dark of face, with restless eyes and sharp, strong features, every part of him defined: small, strong hands, slender arms, and a thin and bony nose.",
                "Behind him walked his opposite, a huge man, shapeless of face, with large, pale eyes, with wide, sloping shoulders, and he walked heavily, dragging his feet a little, the way a bear drags his paws.",
                "The small man's name was George Milton, and the big man's name was Lennie Small, and together they traveled through the California valleys, seeking work on the ranches that dotted the landscape like scattered beads of a broken necklace.",
                "George was the leader, the thinker, the planner, and Lennie was the follower, the worker, the innocent, and their relationship was one of the strangest and most beautiful that I have ever known.",
                "They had been traveling together for years, ever since they were boys, ever since Lennie's aunt Clara had died and left him in George's care, and George had taken on the burden without complaint, or at least without much complaint.",
                "Lennie was not like other men, for his mind had never fully developed, and he saw the world with the simple wonder of a child, without the complications and the cruelties that the rest of us learn to accept.",
                "He loved soft things, loved to touch them, loved to hold them, loved to stroke them, and this love was the source of much of the trouble that followed them wherever they went.",
                "He had once been given a pet mouse, and he had loved it so much that he had squeezed it to death by accident, and then he had hidden it in his pocket, and then he had lost it, and he had never quite recovered from the loss.",
                "George was always telling him not to do such things, not to hold on so tightly, not to love so fiercely, but Lennie could not help himself, for his love was as simple and as overwhelming as a flood.",
                "They sat down by the river, and George began to speak of their dream, the dream that he had told Lennie a hundred times before, the dream that kept them both going through the long and difficult days.",
                "One day, they would have a farm of their own, a small place, with a house and a garden and a few animals, and they would be their own masters, and no one would ever tell them what to do again.",
                "And most important of all, they would have rabbits, lots and lots of rabbits, soft and warm and wonderful, and Lennie would take care of them, and he would never, ever squeeze them too hard.",
                "It was a dream that seemed impossible, for men like George and Lennie never got their own farms, and the world was not kind to dreamers, and yet they dreamed it anyway, because the dream was all they had.",
                "George warned Lennie to stay away from the ranch owner's wife, for he had heard stories about her, stories of a woman who caused trouble, and George did not want any trouble.",
                "He warned Lennie to be careful, to keep his mouth shut, to do his work, and to let George do the talking, and Lennie promised to remember, though George knew that he would forget.",
                "They talked about the past, about the ranches they had worked on, about the people they had met, about the trouble that they had left behind them, and about the hope that kept them moving forward.",
                "They fell asleep in the open field, under the stars, and the night was peaceful and quiet, and for a few hours they were at peace with the world.",
                "In the morning, they would walk to the ranch, and they would meet new people, and they would begin a new chapter in their lives, a chapter that would turn out to be their last.",
                "For the ranch where they were going was not like other ranches, and the people they would meet were not like other people, and the events that would unfold would change everything forever.",
                "The boss was a small man with a suspicious nature, and he did not like the idea of two men traveling together, for he had never seen such a thing before, and he did not trust what he did not understand.",
                "His son, Curley, was a young man with a chip on his shoulder, a man who liked to fight, a man who was jealous of anyone bigger than himself, and he immediately took a dislike to Lennie.",
                "Curley's wife was a beautiful woman with a wandering eye, a woman who was lonely and unhappy, a woman who sought attention wherever she could find it, a woman who would prove to be the undoing of them all.",
                "There was an old man named Candy, who had lost a hand in an accident, and who kept a dog that was old and blind and useless, and Candy feared that he too would soon be discarded.",
                "There was a black man named Crooks, who lived in the barn because the other men would not let him sleep in the bunkhouse, and who had learned to find his comfort in books and in solitude.",
                "And there was a man named Slim, the prince of the ranch, the man who drove the mule team, the man whose opinion was respected by everyone, the man who understood things without being told.",
                "It was to this world that George and Lennie came, and it was in this world that their dream would be tested, and it was in this world that the tragedy would unfold.",
                "For in a world of loneliness and cruelty and desperation, the friendship of George and Lennie was a rare and precious thing, a thing that could not be allowed to last."
            )),
            EnChapter(2, "The Dream and the Dog", listOf(
                "Candy was an old man who had worked on the ranch for many years, and he had seen many men come and go, and he had learned to accept the harsh realities of life on the ranch.",
                "His dog had been his only companion for many years, a sheepdog that had once been a fine animal but was now old and blind and lame, and the other men had begun to say that the dog should be put out of its misery.",
                "Candy did not want to shoot his dog, for the dog was his friend, but the pressure from the other men was too great, and in the end, he agreed, and Carlson took the dog out and shot it in the back of the head.",
                "It was a small thing, a dog's death, but it was also a symbol of something larger, of the way that the world discarded those who had become useless, of the way that the weak were always the first to go.",
                "Candy was devastated by the loss of his dog, but he hid his grief behind a mask of stoic acceptance, for that was how men on the ranch survived, by learning to bury their feelings deep inside.",
                "And then, one evening, he overheard George telling Lennie about their dream, about the farm they were going to own, about the rabbits, about the freedom, about the future.",
                "Candy's ears perked up, for he had money saved, money that he had received as compensation for his lost hand, and he thought that perhaps he could join them, that perhaps he could be part of a dream for once in his life.",
                "He offered them his money, three hundred and fifty dollars, more than most men on the ranch would ever see in their entire lives, and he asked only that he be allowed to join them when they went.",
                "George was suspicious at first, for he had never trusted anyone but Lennie, but the money was real, and the dream was real, and for the first time in his life, the dream began to seem possible.",
                "And so the three of them began to plan, to talk about the farm, to argue about what kind of cows they would raise, to imagine the life that they would build together, to hope.",
                "It was a beautiful dream, and for a few weeks, it seemed as though it might actually come true, and the joy that the dream brought to three lonely men was almost enough to justify the pain that was to come.",
                "But there was trouble on the ranch, trouble in the form of Curley and his wife, and trouble in the form of Lennie's inability to control his own strength.",
                "Curley was always looking for a fight, always looking for someone to humiliate, and he had set his sights on Lennie, for Lennie was big and strong and simple, and Curley thought he would be an easy target.",
                "One day, Curley attacked Lennie, hitting him again and again in the face, and Lennie did not fight back, for George had told him not to fight, and Lennie always tried to do what George told him.",
                "But finally, George told him to fight back, and Lennie grabbed Curley's hand and squeezed it, and he squeezed it so hard that every bone in Curley's hand was crushed.",
                "It was an accident, but it was also a warning, a sign of the power that Lennie possessed and could not control, a sign of the danger that would eventually destroy everything.",
                "Curley was humiliated, and he agreed to keep quiet about what had happened, for he did not want to admit that he had been beaten by a man like Lennie.",
                "But the incident left everyone shaken, and it left George more worried than ever about the future, for he knew that Lennie's strength was like a loaded gun waiting to go off.",
                "And so the weeks passed, and the dream grew, and the danger grew with it, and everyone waited for the moment when the bubble would finally burst.",
                "The moment came on a Sunday afternoon, when the men had gone to town and only a few were left on the ranch, and Lennie was alone in the barn with the body of a puppy that he had accidentally killed.",
                "Curley's wife came to him, seeking conversation, seeking comfort, seeking something that she could not name, and she began to talk to Lennie in a way that she had never talked to anyone else.",
                "She told him about her dreams, about the life she had imagined for herself, about the disappointments that had brought her to this lonely place, and she spoke with a bitter honesty that was almost unbearable to hear.",
                "And then, in a moment of simple kindness, she asked him to feel how soft her hair was, and he did, and he began to stroke it, and he began to hold on too tightly, and she began to struggle.",
                "He held on, afraid of the consequences, afraid of what George would say, afraid of losing the only thing that mattered to him, and she screamed, and he shook her, and her neck broke.",
                "It was an accident, as everything with Lennie was an accident, but the result was the same, and the body of Curley's wife lay on the floor of the barn, and Lennie knew that he had done something terrible.",
                "He fled from the barn, fled from the ranch, fled to the place by the river where George had told him to go if he ever got into trouble, and he waited there, frightened and alone, for George to come.",
                "And George came, as he had always come, and he found Lennie sitting by the river, and he knew what he had to do, and he knew that there was no other way.",
                "For if the men from the ranch found Lennie, they would torture him, they would make him suffer, they would kill him slowly and painfully, and George could not bear the thought of that.",
                "So George told Lennie to look across the river, and he told him the story of the farm one last time, and he described the rabbits and the garden and the house, and Lennie listened and smiled.",
                "And then, while Lennie was lost in the dream, George raised the gun and shot him in the back of the head, and Lennie died without ever knowing what had happened, without ever feeling any pain, without ever losing his dream."
            ))
        )
    )

